## What is Music Generation?

Music generation is the task of producing new musical content, either as raw audio waveforms or as symbolic notation (MIDI). Where transcription maps audio to symbols, generation goes the other direction, and the core challenge shifts from *recognition* to *coherence*: the model must produce output that is acoustically valid and stays musically sensible across seconds or minutes of structure, style, and harmonic motion.

This session covered two papers that approach generation from opposite ends of the representation spectrum. [VampNet (Garcia et al., 2023)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.04686) operates in the **audio domain**, encoding raw waveforms into discrete codec tokens and using a bidirectional masked transformer to generate music through iterative parallel decoding. The [Anticipatory Music Transformer (Thickstun et al., 2023)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.08620) operates in the **symbolic domain**, modeling MIDI events as a temporal point process and introducing an interleaving strategy that enables controllable infilling. Both use transformers, but their conditioning strategies are fundamentally different: VampNet attends bidirectionally (seeing both past and future context), while AMT uses a causal architecture with a novel anticipation mechanism that inserts future control signals into the autoregressive stream.

<Callout type="definition" label="Masked acoustic token modeling">
	Encode the waveform into a sequence of discrete codec tokens, randomly mask some of them, and train a bidirectional transformer to fill in the blanks. At inference, you can hand the model any partial token grid and it will iteratively complete it. This is BERT for audio, with the codec doing the work of "subword tokenization."
</Callout>

<Callout type="insight" label="Bidirectional vs. causal-with-anticipation">
	The deepest split between these two papers is when the model sees future context. VampNet sees it directly (bidirectional attention). AMT stays causal but interleaves *future* control events into the autoregressive stream so the next-token prediction is implicitly conditioned on what's coming. Two routes to the same underlying need: real music is rarely strictly causal.
</Callout>

