## Editing: Variable Length, Continuation, and Audio-to-Audio

The same model that generates from scratch also edits, because conditioning is just another input. These run through the API (`large`).

**Variable length.** Identical prompt and seed, two durations. The 60-second version isn't the 8-second one looped — the model composes for the length it's given. (It's also why the file is roughly 7× larger.)

<div class="sa3-ladder sa3-ladder-2">
	<AudioPlayer src="/audio/stable-audio/sa3_large_vlen-8s.mp3" label="8 seconds" />
	<AudioPlayer src="/audio/stable-audio/sa3_large_vlen-60s.mp3" label="60 seconds" isTarget={true} />
</div>

**Continuation (inpainting).** Take the jazz-piano clip from the ladder, then ask the model to regenerate the region *past its end* — seeded by the original so it stays coherent — with a new instruction to "build into a walking-bass swing groove." Inpainting a causal mask at the tail is exactly continuation.

<div class="sa3-ladder sa3-ladder-2">
	<AudioPlayer src="/audio/stable-audio/sa3_large_jazz-piano.mp3" label="seed (first 12s)" />
	<AudioPlayer src="/audio/stable-audio/sa3_large_continuation.mp3" label="extended to 24s" isTarget={true} />
</div>

**Audio-to-audio.** Feed the cello clip as a structural reference (`strength=0.75`) and re-prompt it as "a warm analog synth pad, dreamy and slow." The melodic contour survives; the timbre is reimagined.

<div class="sa3-ladder sa3-ladder-2">
	<AudioPlayer src="/audio/stable-audio/sa3_large_cello.mp3" label="cello reference" />
	<AudioPlayer src="/audio/stable-audio/sa3_large_a2a.mp3" label="→ synth pad" isTarget={true} />
</div>

## How It All Fits

Lay the three approaches in this post side by side and the design space comes into focus. VampNet quantizes audio into discrete codec tokens and unmasks them bidirectionally. The Anticipatory Music Transformer leaves the audio domain entirely for symbolic events and interleaves controls into a causal stream. Stable Audio 3 keeps a *continuous* latent and denoises it with diffusion. Three representations — discrete-audio, symbolic, continuous-latent — and three modeling paradigms — masked, autoregressive, diffusion.

But the thread from the first half of this post holds. Every one of these systems treats **controllable generation** as a first-class goal, and every one of them leans on a *learned compression* of audio to make modeling tractable: VampNet on DAC's residual quantizer, SA3 on the SAME autoencoder. The shift from VampNet to SAME is the shift from *discrete and acoustic* to *continuous and semantic* — a latent designed not just to reconstruct the waveform but to be aligned with text and easy to generate. That alignment is why a 459M model can do, on a laptop, what would have been a server-class job a couple of years ago.

<Callout type="insight" label="The takeaway">
	The autoencoder is not a preprocessing detail — it's half the model. SAME spends real effort making its latent semantically structured and generatable, and Stable Audio 3 cashes that in as speed, small model sizes, and variable-length, editable generation. When you're evaluating a modern generative-audio system, ask what its latent space was optimized for, not just how big the generator is.
</Callout>

## Run Stable Audio 3 Yourself

The reading-group repo includes an `experiments/stable-audio` project that reproduces every clip above. `generate.py` runs the open-weight `small-music` and `medium` tiers locally (Apple Silicon or CUDA) via `stable-audio-tools`; `api_demos.py` drives the `large` tier and the editing demos through the Stability API. The same prompt list and per-prompt seeds are shared across both so the tiers line up for A/B listening.

<div class="run-box">

```bash
cd music-ai-reading-group/experiments/stable-audio
./setup.sh                                  # uv env + the numpy/pywavelets + lightning fixes

# Local open-weight tiers (gated weights — needs an HF token with SA3 access):
export HF_TOKEN=hf_...
uv run --no-sync generate.py --model small  --duration 12
uv run --no-sync generate.py --model medium --duration 12

# Large tier + editing demos via the API (needs STABILITY_API_KEY):
export STABILITY_API_KEY=...
uv run --no-sync api_demos.py --what all
```

</div>

<p class="muted">Two gotchas the setup script handles for you: <code>stable-audio-tools</code> can resolve a numpy/pywavelets pair with mismatched binary interfaces (which crashes at import), and it imports <code>pytorch_lightning</code> at model-build time without always declaring it. Always invoke with <code>uv run --no-sync</code> so a re-sync doesn't undo those pins. First load downloads the T5Gemma text encoder and the weights; after that, generation is seconds.</p>

## Run VampNet Yourself

Two scripts are provided. `dac_experiments.py` reconstructs audio at each of the 9 codebook levels so you can hear the RVQ hierarchy. `vampnet_experiments.py` runs VampNet with four masking strategies (vamping, dense vamping, inpainting, beat-driven). Both require Python 3.9–3.11 and about 2 GB of disk space for model weights.

<div class="run-box">

```bash
# Install uv (Python package manager)
curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh

# Clone and set up
git clone https://github.com/mclemcrew/music-ai-reading-group.git
cd music-ai-reading-group/experiments/vampnet

# One-time setup (installs deps, downloads ~1.5 GB of models)
./setup.sh

# DAC codec quality comparison
uv run dac_experiments.py --input path/to/audio.wav

# VampNet masking experiments (wild = creative, faithful = polished)
uv run vampnet_experiments.py --input path/to/audio.wav --style wild
uv run vampnet_experiments.py --input path/to/audio.wav --style faithful
```

</div>

